## Code of Conduct

This project and everyone participating in it is governed by the [Contributor Covenant Code of Conduct](https://www.contributor-covenant.org/version/2/1/code_of_conduct/). By participating, you are expected to uphold this code. Please report unacceptable behavior to [matiwoskebede01@gmail.com](mailto:matiwoskebede01@gmail.com).

## Ways to Contribute

We welcome contributions of all kinds. Here are some ways you can help:

- **Report bugs** – if you find a bug, please let us know by opening an issue.
- **Suggest enhancements** – have an idea for a new feature or improvement? Open an issue and tell us.
- **Improve documentation** – if you find any part of the documentation confusing or incomplete, please help us improve it.
- **Submit pull requests** – whether it's a bug fix, a new feature, or a documentation update, we welcome your code.
- **Share the project** – if you find OpenScanVision useful, please share it with others and star it on GitHub.


## Reporting Bugs

If you find a bug, please open an issue on GitHub with the following information:

- **Title** – a clear, concise description of the issue.
- **Description** – what happened, what you expected to happen, and steps to reproduce.
- **Environment** – device model, Android version, camera used, lighting conditions, card type.
- **Logs** – any relevant logcat output or error messages.
- **Screenshots** – if applicable, attach screenshots of the issue.

Please search the existing issues before opening a new one to avoid duplicates.


## Suggesting Enhancements

We are always looking to improve OpenScanVision. If you have an idea for a new feature or enhancement, please open an issue with:

- **Title** – a clear, concise description of your suggestion.
- **Description** – what problem it solves, how it works, and why it would be valuable.
- **Alternatives** – any alternative solutions you have considered.

We will review your suggestion and provide feedback as soon as possible.


## Development Setup

To set up the project for local development:

### Prerequisites
- Android Studio (latest version recommended)
- JDK 11 or higher
- Android SDK with API 24 or higher
- A physical Android device with a camera (emulators are not recommended)

### Clone the Repository
```bash
git clone https://github.com/MatiwosKebede/OpenScanVision.git
cd OpenScanVision
```

### Build the Project
```bash
./gradlew clean build
```

### Run the Sample App
1. Open the project in Android Studio.
2. Select the `sample` module as the run target.
3. Connect a physical device and run the app.

### Run Tests
```bash
./gradlew test
```


## Pull Request Guidelines

When submitting a pull request, please follow these guidelines:

### Step 1: Fork the Repository
Fork the repository on GitHub and clone your fork locally.

### Step 2: Create a Feature Branch
Create a new branch for your feature or bug fix:
```bash
git checkout -b feature/your-feature-name
```

### Step 3: Make Your Changes
Make your changes, following the [code style guidelines](#code-style).

### Step 4: Test Your Changes
Run the tests to ensure your changes don't break anything:
```bash
./gradlew clean build
```
If you've added new functionality, please add tests.

### Step 5: Commit Your Changes
Write a clear commit message (see [commit messages](#commit-messages) below).

### Step 6: Push to Your Fork
```bash
git push origin feature/your-feature-name
```

### Step 7: Open a Pull Request
1. Go to the main repository on GitHub.
2. Click on "Pull Requests" and then "New Pull Request".
3. Select your branch from the compare dropdown.
4. Fill in the pull request template (if applicable) and provide a clear description of your changes.
5. Submit the pull request.

### What We Look For in Pull Requests
- **Clear description** – explain what you changed and why.
- **Focused changes** – keep each pull request focused on a single topic.
- **No breaking changes** – unless absolutely necessary, ensure backward compatibility.
- **Updated documentation** – if you change the API, update the documentation accordingly.
- **Added tests** – new features should include tests.


## Code Style

We follow the official [Kotlin coding conventions](https://kotlinlang.org/docs/coding-conventions.html). Here are some key points:

- **Indentation** – use 4 spaces, not tabs.
- **Naming** – use `CamelCase` for classes and functions; `snake_case` for constants.
- **Imports** – organise imports alphabetically.
- **No unused imports** – remove any unused imports.
- **Comments** – use `//` for line comments and `/** */` for KDoc.

We use [ktlint](https://github.com/pinterest/ktlint) to enforce code style. You can run it locally before committing:
```bash
./gradlew ktlintCheck
```


## Testing

We strongly encourage writing tests for any new functionality or bug fixes. The project uses:

- **Unit tests** – for testing OMR, QR, and card detection logic (under `src/test`).
- **Instrumented tests** – for testing camera integration and UI (under `src/androidTest`).

### Running Tests
```bash
# Run unit tests
./gradlew test

# Run instrumented tests (requires a connected device)
./gradlew connectedAndroidTest
```

If you add new functionality, please add tests to cover it. If you fix a bug, consider adding a test that reproduces the issue and verifies the fix.


## Commit Messages

We follow the [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) specification for commit messages. This helps automate versioning and changelog generation.

### Format
```
<type>(<scope>): <subject>

[optional body]

[optional footer]
```

### Types
- `feat` – a new feature.
- `fix` – a bug fix.
- `docs` – documentation only changes.
- `style` – changes that do not affect the meaning of the code (whitespace, formatting, etc.).
- `refactor` – a code change that neither fixes a bug nor adds a feature.
- `perf` – a code change that improves performance.
- `test` – adding missing tests or correcting existing tests.
- `chore` – changes to the build process or auxiliary tools.

### Examples
```
feat: add multi-frame averaging for improved accuracy
```
```
fix: resolve crash when QR code is not detected
```
```
docs: update README with library integration instructions
```


## Getting Help

If you have any questions about the project or the contribution process, please:

- Open an [issue on GitHub](https://github.com/MatiwosKebede/OpenScanVision/issues).
- Contact the maintainer directly at [matiwoskebede01@gmail.com](mailto:matiwoskebede01@gmail.com).

We try to respond within 48 hours.


